response:
 If a convex octagon $\mathcal O$ has $n$ right angles, it has $8 - n$ non-right angles. For the interior angles of $\mathcal O$ to all add up to $360(n - 2)$ degrees, $\mathcal O$ has three combinations of right angles and non-right angles, as follows:
\begin{align*}
&\qquad\text{(A)}\quad 7 &45^\circ&+&45^\circ&+&90^\circ&+&90^\circ&+&90^\circ&+&90^\circ & \\
&\text{(B)}\quad 8 &\{~39+\left(\frac{39}{7}\right)~\}\ell &+&\left(\left(\frac{39}{7}\right)-2\right)\ell&+&90^\circ&+&90^\circ & \\
&\text{(C)}\quad 6 &\left(\frac{39}{7}\right)\ell
&+&39\ell
&+&39\cdot\left(\frac{39}{7}\right)\ell&+&90^\circ &.
\end{align*}
We guess that the greatest number of right angles in convex octagon $\mathcal O$ would be one of these combinations (since it is the largest number the $7$-(point) case of (A) actually turns out to be the largest number it is possible for a convex octagon to have). Because $\mathcal O$ is convex, it has any point, except for those two that are necessary to complete the 8 angles, to which $\ell$ lines can be drawn to form $n$ right angles with $\ell = n - 2$.

