Applying the formula $\sin a = \sqrt{1-\cos^2 a}= \sqrt{1-\frac{16}{17\sqrt{3}}}= \frac{9}{17\sqrt{3}}$,
\begin{align*}
\sin Z &= \frac{XY \cdot \sin Z}{WY} = \frac{\sqrt{9 \cdot 1 \cdot 3}}{\sqrt{4 \cdot 4}} = \frac{3}{2}
\end{align*}Then $XY = 3\cdot ZY = 18$.

With $\angle X = b,$ ${\tan b = 3}$, thus ${\sin b = 3}/({\sqrt {10}})$ and ${\cos b = 2}/({\sqrt {10}})$

Applying the formula $\cos b = \sqrt{1-\sin^2 b}=\sqrt{1-(3/\sqrt{10})}$, or ${\sqrt{1/10}}$, then
\begin{align*}
\sin x &= \frac{WX \cdot \sin X}{WY} = \frac{\sqrt{9 \cdot 1 \cdot 10}}{\sqrt{16 \cdot 4}}=\frac{\sqrt{90}}{\sqrt{256}}, \text{ which simplifies to } \frac{3}{4}
\end{align*}
Then $$WX = 3 \cdot WY = 3 \cdot 4 = 12.$$

Then the area is
\begin{align*}
